Secret patterns from gitleaks, https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks, file `config/gitleaks.toml`
at commit 5f6ec56cfa6f. The upstream file's sha256 is
e163e53b9e7e8a8511e77271e2b323ed057759542a6d988258afe3a1fa329caf.

`gitleaks.json` is that file converted to JSON, mechanically and losslessly, with Python's
standard `tomllib`: an object whose `global` is the file's `[allowlist]` table and whose `rules`
is its `[[rules]]` array, each value as the TOML holds it. The converter is not part of this
repository; its output's sha256 was
250c8ddb50df41faf1347884440ca5c30ed3de2999796222767d3c7924eae60b.

Three things were then taken out, so that no string in the shape of a provider's key stands in
this repository, where a secret scanner would take it for one: the allowlist of `gcp-api-key`,
which is sixteen example Google API keys; the UUID among the global allowlist's stopwords; and the
32-digit hexadecimal number among the stopwords of `generic-api-key`. Without them more is cut,
never less. Nothing else differs. `../SHA256SUMS` holds the sha256 of `gitleaks.json` as it is
here, and a test fails when the file differs from it; the program checks the same sum each time it
loads the set, and loads nothing when it differs.

The program evaluates the 217 rules that are not bound to a file path: their keywords, regular
expression, secret group, entropy threshold, and the regular expressions and stopwords of their
allowlists and of the global one. It does not take the `gitleaks:allow` comment, the decoding
passes, or the path allowlists. It never validates a secret.

